pears to have been adviser-framed rather than client-articulated.","The risk level 4 (Cautious Balanced) is stated as the agreed level in the report; P11 records no additional notes on how the agreed risk level was reached, and P1 confirms the ATRQ was not completed at the meeting — the client completed it independently on Defaqto, so there is no in-meeting corroboration of the agreed risk level discussion captured in the risk profile report.","The report's retirement age assumption of 60 is used in the critical yield calculation; P8 records the adviser questioning 'Defo 60?' — suggesting some uncertainty about this figure at the time of the meeting, though P3 and P9 both record target age 60.","The report uses modelled expenditure of £2,206/month, consistent with the fact find total (P5: £2,206.67); however the report's 'Your current situation' section states £2,493 essential plus £800 discretionary — the higher figure is not reflected in the cashflow modelling, and no explanation is given for the discrepancy (noted in extraction ambiguities but not addressed in the report narrative)."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The core retirement objectives — target age 60, minimum income of £2,200 net per month, review of Aviva pension — are well-evidenced in the fact find (P3, P9) and adviser notes (P7, P8). The inheritance investment goal and tax-efficiency motive are supported by P3 and P7. These key objectives have clear client authorship in the file. However, several issues arise. First, the Cash ISA transfer is presented in the report as a client objective when the file records it as highly tentative ('not dead set', 'not likely though' — P3, P7); this overstates client intent. Second, the platform consolidation objective (objective 4) is not recorded anywhere in the fact find or adviser notes as a client-expressed preference; it reads as an adviser-framed efficiency benefit, not a stated client goal, and calling it isClientStated=true is unsupported. Third, the planned £40k spend on kitchen/bathroom (P7) is a foreseeable capital deployment that affects the net investable sum and should be reflected in assumptions or objectives but is absent from the report. Fourth, while the retirement age of 60 is recorded in the fact find, the adviser's own note (P8 'Defo 60?') signals it was not firmly established at the time of the meeting — the report treats it as settled without acknowledging this uncertainty. Fifth, the expenditure discrepancy (£2,206 modelled vs £2,493 essential + £800 discretionary stated elsewhere in the report) is an internal inconsistency already identified in the extraction; it means the cashflow modelling understates expenditure relative to the report's own narrative, which is relevant to whether the retirement income objective can be met. The risk level agreement process has no supporting notes in the risk profile document (P11) and the ATRQ was completed by the client independently without adviser oversight documented in the file. The guards apply as follows: the retirement age and income target are evidenced in the fact find and are not merely inferred; the consolidation objective, however, is not evidenced even as an inferred client preference. The Cash ISA transfer is evidenced but as tentative, not as a firm objective. The kitchen/bathroom spend (foreseeable life change) is in the file but absent from the report. Overall, several objectives are adequately evidenced, but two are either overstated (consolidation as client-stated) or inadequately qualified (Cash ISA transfer), and one foreseeable material capital use is unaddressed.","citations":[{"passageId":"P3","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"Wants to be fully retired by 60. Min income in retirement is £2,200 net per month. Also looking at investment advice on £60k of his Dad's property money coming in this month, and the £40k cash he currently has so as to avoid paying tax on interest. He has already used his full ISA allowance this year, so has his wife. Potentially interested in transferring his cash ISA to S&S ISA but would have to be really worth his while - not dead set on doing this.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Notes - Barnes.md","category":"C","quote":"About to receive circa £100k but will use £40k on kitchen/bathroom.\nHas another £40k in high interest savings, but only look at investing the £60k. Possibly move ISAs – not likely though. Only ATR 4.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Notes - Barnes.md","category":"C","quote":"Retired, gets DB income, but works part-time. When will he stop? Defo 60?","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["£2,200.00","Monthly","60","Medium Term"]},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Calculated Total Monthly Household Expenditure","£ 2,206.67"]},{"passageId":"P11","source":"Risk profile report 11 08 2025 08 58.md","category":"D","quote":"No additional notes were recorded","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"Client did not have time to complete an ATRQ today, so have sent him an email version to complete himself on Defaqto.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Missing evidence","Weak justification","Disclosure shortfall"],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

