ere. The inconsistency itself as a factual matter is a discrepancy.\n\n5. ASSUMPTIONS NOT CORROBORATED IN CASHFLOW FILES: Savings growth rate 1.25% and Aviva Platform investments growth rate 4.5% are asserted in the report but the retrieved cashflow file passages do not include investment return rates (only inflation assumptions are visible in the retrieved passages). The cashflow files that were retrieved (market crash, Tony/Yvette predeceases) do not show these growth rates. This is an absence of corroboration rather than a contradiction. However, given the cashflow files do confirm other overlapping assumptions (inflation rates, surplus saved %, 2028 return override), the absence of investment return rates in the passages is likely a retrieval gap rather than the files being silent — but on what is available, these two rates are unconfirmed.\n\n6. DB PENSION, EMPLOYMENT INCOME, STATE PENSION INPUTS: These key income figures fed into the cashflow (£22,000, £28,000, £11,973 each) are not directly confirmed by any retrieved cashflow file passage. The cashflow files retrieved do not show an income schedule. This is a retrieval gap; the files are present but the income input pages were not retrieved.\n\nThe expenditure inconsistency (item 1) and the charge arithmetic inconsistency (item 2) are the material findings. The expenditure discrepancy affects whether the cashflow correctly represents the clients' spending and therefore whether the no-shortfall conclusion is valid. The charge inconsistency affects the switch comparison. Both are internal to the report and need no external document to establish.","citations":[{"passageId":"P6","source":"Cashflow - Market Crash.md","category":"F","quote":null,"cells":["GENERAL 2 %","PROPERTY 4.7 %","STATE PENSION 2.5 %","LENDING 4 %"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Cashflow - Market Crash.md","category":"F","quote":"Returns overridden  for Investments (excluding cash); Pensions for the following years: -30% in 2028","cells":null},