{"groupId":"G5.1","requirement":"Affordability of initial and ongoing commitments — consistency between what the suitability report asserts and what the case file evidences.","reportSays":"The report asserts that the initial commitment of £2,880 net (£3,600 gross) single pension contribution is affordable from inheritance funds without affecting the client's lifestyle. It further asserts that current earnings are sufficient to support the recommended contribution level and that joint expenditure is covered leaving surplus income each month. Monthly expenditure is stated in the 'Your current situation' section as approximately £2,493 (essential) plus £800 discretionary; the cashflow modelling inputs section states approximately £2,206 per month. The report claims affordability is supported by inheritance funds (£100,000 due or received) and a healthy liquid asset base. No ongoing regular contribution commitment is created — the contribution is a single net payment of £2,880.","fileSays":"The cashflow files (P1, P3, P4, P7) show combined first-year income of £38,800 (Anthony DB pension £22,000 + Yvette salary £16,800) and total regular expenditure of £29,915 in year one, producing a positive cashflow. Liquid assets at outset are shown as £225,018. The cashflow modelling uses an expenditure figure consistent with approximately £2,206/month (£29,915 / 12 ≈ £2,493/month in annual terms per the income statement in P7, but the cashflow inputs section references £2,206/month — see extraction ambiguity). An emergency fund of £10,000 cash is identified as a separate ring-fenced item liquidated last (P3, P4). The cashflow scenarios (base, market crash, Tony predeceases, Yvette predeceases) all show liquid assets remaining positive throughout life. The GIA illustration (P19) confirms the initial adviser charge of £4,794.37 from the single payments. The contribution is a one-off single payment of £2,880 net, not a recurring income commitment.","discrepancies":["Monthly expenditure is stated in the report's 'Your current situation' section as approximately £2,493 (essential) plus £800 discretionary, but the cashflow modelling inputs section within the same report states approximately £2,206 per month — a difference of £287/month that is unexplained. The cashflow model annual expenditure of £29,915 (P7) divided by 12 equates to approximately £2,493/month, which is closer to the higher figure, creating further confusion about which figure was actually used as the modelling input.","The extraction flags an ambiguity over whether the £100,000 inheritance had been received at report date (16/12/2025): the narrative says it was 'due this month' implying not yet received, but the balance sheet includes it at full value (£190,000 total). The report's affordability claim rests materially on these inheritance funds.","The report states 'your current earnings are sufficient to support the recommended contribution level' without quantifying current-year earnings, while elsewhere acknowledging uncertainty about this tax year's earnings due to retirement followed by a brief part-time role. This leaves the earnings basis for the £3,600 gross pension contribution unverified in the file evidence.","The cashflow model inputs section references approximately £2,206/month expenditure whereas the first-year expenditure statement (P7) totals £29,915 annually (≈£2,493/month), yet P1 shows Total Money Out in 2025 as £29,915 — the cashflow model appears to use the higher annual figure regardless of the inputs section narrative figure."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Confirmed current tax-year earnings for Anthony to verify the £3,600 gross pension contribution is within earnings or the non-earner allowance — the report acknowledges uncertainty but does not resolve it with a figure.","Confirmation of whether the £100,000 inheritance had actually been received at the report date, to verify the stated liquid asset base of £190,000."],"analysis":"The single most material affordability question here is whether the £2,880 net / £3,600 gross one-off pension contribution and the total initial advice fee of £4,794 are affordable from the client's financial position. The cashflow files (P1, P3, P4, P7) are consistent in showing liquid assets at outset of approximately £225,000, first-year combined income of £38,800 and first-year expenditure of £29,915, leaving a comfortable surplus and substantial liquid reserves. On this basis the one-off contribution presents no affordability challenge. The emergency fund of £10,000 is separately ring-fenced and liquidated last in all scenarios. The first guard applies: the one-off transfer and associated fees are drawn from capital, not from income, and should not be tested against monthly surplus. The ongoing commitment is nil from an income perspective — there is no regular contribution. \n\nHowever, two concerns remain. First, the expenditure figure inconsistency is a genuine data quality issue: the 'Your current situation' section cites ~£2,493/month essential expenditure (plus £800 discretionary) while the cashflow modelling inputs section cites ~£2,206/month — a £287/month difference that is not explained within the report. The annual cashflow output of £29,915 (P7/P1) is consistent with ~£2,493/month, suggesting the modelling used the higher figure and the '£2,206' inputs reference may be an error or exclude a component, but this is not stated. This is a data quality/consistency issue flagged by the extraction as an internal inconsistency. It does not undermine the affordability conclusion materially — even at £2,493/month the surplus is positive — but it is a disclosure and data quality concern. \n\nSecond, the report's earnings sufficiency assertion is unsupported by evidence in the file. The report itself acknowledges uncertainty about current-year earnings and never quantifies them. The contribution could be justified under the £3,600 non-earner allowance, but the report claims it is justified by earnings sufficiency without providing the earnings figure. No fact-find or file note in the evidence pack corroborates a specific current-year earnings figure. This is a weak justification and a missing evidence concern, though it does not affect the affordability conclusion for the one-off contribution itself. \n\nThe inheritance ambiguity (£100,000 'due this month' vs already included in the balance sheet) is a data quality concern but does not fundamentally undermine affordability: even without the inheritance the client holds £90,000 liquid plus cashflow-positive income, and the one-off contribution of £2,880 is easily manageable. No vulnerability indicators in the file appear to have bearing on this affordability check specifically. Overall the affordability conclusion is likely sound but the expenditure figure inconsistency and the unquantified earnings basis are material data quality and weak justification concerns that require clarification.","citations":[{"passageId":"P12","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"£2,880 net (£3,600 gross) single","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"This is an amount you can comfortably afford from your inheritance funds without affecting your current lifestyle.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"your current earnings are sufficient to support the recommended contribution level","cells":null},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Cashflow - Yvette Predeceases in 2040.md","category":"F","quote":null,"cells":["DB Pension","22,000","0","22,000"]},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Cashflow - Yvette Predeceases in 2040.md","category":"F","quote":null,"cells":["Employment","0","16,800","16,800"]},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Cashflow - Yvette Predeceases in 2040.md","category":"F","quote":null,"cells":["Total Regular Income","22,000","16,800","38,800"]},{"passageId":"P7",