rmed in the pack that it was received and reviewed before the advice was given, which calls into question whether the risk profile underpinning the investment rationale was established from a completed questionnaire.","The report's rationale for the pension contribution (REC3) states current earnings are sufficient but does not quantify them; the extraction flags ambiguity about whether this is based on confirmed earnings or the non-earner £3,600 allowance — leaving the earnings basis of the contribution rationale unsupported by documentary evidence in the pack.","The ISA transfer rationale acknowledges the client was 'not dead set' on transfer (per fact find [P4]) yet the recommendation proceeds — the report's personalised framing of this as meeting the client's objective requires stronger linkage to the stated conditional preference."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Completed ATRQ confirming the client's risk profile before advice was given","Confirmed current-year earnings figure supporting the pension contribution recommendation (REC3) on an earnings rather than non-earner basis"],"analysis":"The report does contain substantial personalised language tied to Anthony Barnes's stated objectives — retirement at 60, £2,200/month net income, tax-efficiency on inherited proceeds, pension review — and these are corroborated by the fact find [P4] and policy research [P13]. For the majority of recommendations the client-specific linkage is present and supported. However, several concerns arise. First, 5 of 18 rationale items are acknowledged as non-client-specific within the canonical model itself; while individually these may be standard regulatory caveats, the pattern across five items suggests sections of generic boilerplate remain. Second, the monetary cost comparison underpinning the pension switch rationale contains a demonstrable internal inconsistency: the switch charges table implies a new annual charge of £490 while the charges table states £512.69