iva Platform [P12–P18, P21], so this benefit does not fully apply to the pension switch element — the pension is not being moved to a new platform, it is being restructured within the same platform. The ISA and GIA transfers do represent genuine integration, but the report presents this as a uniform benefit across the recommendation without distinguishing which products genuinely benefit.","The report relies on 'Professional Oversight' to justify the cost increase but no independent evidence in the file corroborates the service proposition or confirms a client agreement specifying the 1% ongoing charge. The service description appears only within the suitability report itself [P22], which cannot self-corroborate.","The critical yield achievability assertion ('I believe this is achievable given the improved risk alignment and professional oversight') is made against past performance of the recommended fund. The performance table in the report [P20] shows the existing strategy outperforming the recommended fund over all periods shown (45.28% vs 30.12% over 5 years), which the report acknowledges but then qualifies by reference to volatility. No provider illustration or independent projection is in the pack to support the critical yield claim independently.","The derived figures show that 0.08% on £78,750 = £63 (the switch charge table basis) and 0.62% on £82,691.94 = £512.69 (the charges table basis), implying the two tables use different fund values as their base. The report does not explain why the fund value used in the switch charges assessment (implied ~£78,750) differs from the fund value implied by the new plan charges table (~£82,692). This is an unexplained internal inconsistency in the charge arithmetic."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Provider illustration for the new Aviva Platform Personal Pension (NP1) showing projected values and RIY — noted as absent in extraction path /solution/NP1/illustration","Client agreement or fee disclosure document independently corroborating the 1% ongoing adviser charge and the attached service proposition","Key features document charge schedule for the recommended Aviva Multi-asset Plus II fund confirming the 0.41% investment management charge independently of the suitability report"],"analysis":"The requirement is whether the benefits relied upon to justify the higher cost are evidenced and consistent. Several issues arise.\n\n1. LOWER COSTS BENEFIT: The report presents a platform charge reduction (0.32% → 0.21%) as a 'Lower Costs' benefit in the benefits section, but this is misleading in isolation: the total annual charge increases from 0.54% to 0.62% because the investment management charge on the new fund (0.41%) exceeds the existing fund charge (0.22%). The benefits section does not make clear that the platform saving is more than offset by a higher fund charge. The 'Like for Like' section does address the net 0.08% increase, but the juxtaposition of a 'Lower Costs' benefit label against a net cost increase without immediate qualification in the same section is a disclosure weakness.\n\n2. MONETARY CHARGE INCONSISTENCY: The switch charges assessment table implies the new pension annual charge is £490 (£427 + £63), but the annual recurring charges table states £512.69. The derived figures confirm £512.69 is the arithmetically correct figure at 0.62% on ~£82,692. The £63 increment appears to be computed on a slightly different base (~£78,750 vs ~£82,692). This is an unexplained internal inconsistency, not merely rounding, and overstates the apparent cost benefit of the comparison by making the increase look smaller (£63 vs £85.69 actual difference).\n\n3. RISK ALIGNMENT BENEFIT: This is the strongest benefit. The research report [P5] confirms the agreed risk level of 4 and the existing fund risk level of 6 is documented in the suitability report [P21] and corroborated by the transaction history showing Vanguard LifeStrategy 60% and 80% equity funds. The performance analysis in [P20] provides comparative data. This benefit has genuine support in the file, albeit the supporting performance comparison comes from the report itself [P20] rather than an independent document.\n\n4. PROFESSIONAL OVERSIGHT BENEFIT: The service proposition appears only within the suitability report [P22]. No client agreement or independent fee disclosure document is in the pack. The report cannot self-corroborate. This benefit is asserted to justify the cost increase without independent evidential support in the file — this is the most significant gap.\n\n5. PLATFORM INTEGRATION BENEFIT: The existing pension is already on the Aviva Platform [P12–P18, P21]. Moving pension funds within the same platform is restructuring, not integration. The benefit is real for the ISA and GIA (which are new to the platform), but the report does not distinguish. Consolidation convenience is noted as a real but non-financial benefit per the guard; it is accordingly weighed as stated.\n\n6. CRITICAL YIELD ACHIEVABILITY: The critical yield of 2.94% is stated as achievable based on past performance, but the performance table the report relies on [P20] shows the existing strategy outperforming the recommended fund across all periods. The report addresses this by reference to volatility, which is a plausible qualitative argument, but no independent projection or RIY document is present. The achievability assertion is weakly supported.\n\nVulnerability overlay: No vulnerability assessment is present in the pack (/vulnerability/assessment absent per extraction). No vulnerability indicators in the retrieved passages affect this specific cost/benefit assessment.\n\nOverall: The 'Lower Costs' label against a net cost increase (partially explained but not clearly in the benefits section), the monetary inconsistency between the two cost tables (£490 implied vs £512.69 stated), and the absence of independent corroboration of the Professional Oversight service proposition collectively c