ain circumstances; disinvestment to cover charges if insufficient cash. The Aviva ISA Key Features discloses: money invested can go down as well as up and may be worth less than invested; restrictions on ISA allowance; flexible withdrawal rights. The Aviva GIA Key Features discloses: value can go down as well as up; tax may be payable on transfer; GIA forms part of the estate on death; risks from emerging markets, smaller companies and currency movements. The Aviva fund factsheet discloses: specific risk flags including foreign exchange risk, emerging markets, smaller companies, fixed interest, high yield bonds, property, alternative investment funds, derivatives. The policy research file confirms the existing plan details. The fact find records no vulnerability flags, no accessible format requirements, and notes the client did not complete an ATRQ at the meeting. No reduction-in-yield illustration or explicit loss scenario (e.g. projected fund value under stress or lower growth rate) is present in the evidence pack.","discrepancies":["The report states all other risks are detailed in product literature, wholly delegating specific product and investment risks (e.g. currency, emerging markets, drawdown depletion, death benefit tax treatment post-75) to the product literature rather than explaining them in the report itself. The product literature confirms these risks exist but they are not summarised in the report.","No explicit loss scenario or reduction-in-yield projection is present in the report or anywhere in the evidence pack. The report does not illustrate a stressed or lower-growth outcome to show how the client's fund or income could be adversely affected.","The report's outperformance requirement calculation contains an internal inconsistency noted in the extraction: the switch charges table implies a new plan annual charge of £490 (£427+£63), whereas the annual recurring charges table states £512.69. The report does not explain or reconcile this difference