ing the new solution underperforms the existing pension by £0 at low and mid growth rates and £0 at high growth rate when adviser charges are included in the comparison — this set of figures (inclusive of adviser charge) appears in the switch report but no equivalent client-facing illustration of projected outcomes including the adviser charge is cited in the suitability report's risk/disadvantage disclosures.","The report states the total cost including adviser fee is approximately 1.62% annually versus 0.54% for the current arrangement, but the derived figures note a monetary inconsistency: £427 + £63 = £490 implied new plan charge vs £512.69 stated in the charges table — this internal inconsistency in the monetary quantum of the cost disadvantage is not flagged to the client."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report makes a genuine and reasonably detailed attempt at risk and disadvantage disclosure: it covers the higher charge differential, total cost including adviser fee, ISA variable rate risk, IHT 2027 change, death-after-75 tax, liquidity delay, cancellation risks and nomination uncertainty. These are client-specific and materially complete for the core pension switch recommendation. The switch report in the file confirms no benefits are lost on switching [P3] ('No benefits were listed for the existing products'), so no omission arises there. The death-after-75 disclosure in the report aligns with the Aviva Key Features [P10] which confirms benefits are taxed at beneficiaries' marginal rate. The projected maturity value comparison is present in the switch report [P3][P5][P14] and shows minimal switching impact at the product-only level (£0–£100 decrease), which the report characterises as a £63 year-one required outperformance — this is an acceptable summary though not a full scenario illustration in the body of the report itself. The main residual concern is the CT Universal MAP KIID's explicit investor-horizon warning: 'this fund may not be appropriate for investors who plan to withdraw their money within 5 years' [P4]. This is a product-specific suitability caveat from the KIID of one of the recommended GIA/ISA funds. Given the client is recently retired and the report addresses access needs and income objectives, this is a client-relevant limitation that should appear in the report rather than being buried in product literature with only a generic delegation sentence covering it. The fund-specific risk warnings (Foreign Exchange, Emerging Markets, Derivatives) from the Aviva factsheet and CT KIID are more marginal — they are standard multi-asset fund risks and the generic delegation to product literature, while not ideal, is a known practice; the guards caution against requiring verbatim repetition of generic market risk. However the 5-year withdrawal horizon warning is a specific suitability caveat, not generic market risk. The monetary inconsistency in the charges (£490 implied vs £512.69 stated) is an internal inconsistency noted in the extraction metadata and is assessed under CHK-007; it does not constitute an undisclosed risk per se but does mean the monetary quantum of the cost disadvantage disclosed to the client may be understated. On balance, the 5-year withdrawal horizon omission from the CT Universal MAP KIID is a disclosure shortfall for a product-specific limitation, even though the overall disclosure standard is reasonable. No vulnerability-specific factors are identified in the evidence that would heighten this concern.","citations":[{"passageId":"P4","source":"CT Universal MAP Cautious C Acc - KIID.md","category":"H","quote":"Recommendation: this fund may not be appropriate for investors who plan to withdraw their money within 5 years.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus II S14 - Factsheet.md","category":"H","quote":null,"cells":["A - General","Yes"]},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus II S14 - F