h costs only £63 more per year and is justified — the switch report's own comparison shows a small projected disadvantage at mid and high growth rates, which the report does not address.","The report asserts that existing funds carry risk level 6 (overall) and that 'one fund is aligned to risk profile 5 and the other to risk profile 7' — this internal inconsistency (noted in the extraction) means the rejection rationale for Option 1 oscillates between a blended risk level of 6 and individual fund risk levels of 5 and 7, potentially weakening the stated mismatch with risk level 4."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Explicit standalone rejection rationale for Option 2 (in-situ fund switch only) with a quantified cost/benefit comparison versus the recommended route","Clarification of whether the employer pension scheme entry in the fact find relates to Anthony Barnes or Yvette Barnes, and whether Anthony has access to an active employer scheme","Explanation of why the pension switch report models the existing pension as 100% Aviva Insured Funds Multi-asset Plus Fund II rather than the Vanguard LifeStrategy 60%/80% funds shown in the review report and suitability report"],"analysis":"The report considers six alternatives and provides specific rejection reasons for each. The primary rationale — risk misalignment (existing funds at risk level 6 against agreed profile of 4) and the cost reduction available from the agency move — is corroborated by the pension switching research report [P16] confirming risk level 4 and the suitability report [P5] confirming the platform charge reduction from 0.32% to 0.21%. The benefits section of the pension switch report [P6] explicitly records no benefits lost on switching, and no transfer penalties apply [P2]. These are the highest-severity concerns (safeguarded benefits and transfer penalties) and both are addressed and supported by evidence.\n\nHowever, three issues require noting. First, the pension switch report [P1] models the existing pension as 100% invested in the Aviva Insured Funds Multi-asset Plus Fund II, yet the personal pension plan review [P14] and the suitability report consistently describe the existing holdings as Vanguard LifeStrategy 60% and 80%. This discrepancy means the like-for-like projections in [P2] and [P6] may not have used the correct existing fund, undermining the reliability of the switching comparison. The switch report's projection comparison showing £0 effect of switching at low growth and only £100 disadvantage at mid/high growth may be based on incorrect existing fund assumptions. Second, the rejection of Option 2 (in-situ fund switch) is inferred rather than explicitly stated and is not quantified. The report lists the agency move as reducing the platform charge from 0.32% to 0.21% (saving 0.11% = approximately £87 per annum on the current fund value), but the recommended fund costs 0.08% more than the existing fund, giving a net saving of approximately 0.03%. Option 2 would achieve the risk-alignment benefit without incurring the higher fund charge, and the report does not explicitly quantify why the full agency move is preferable to Option 2 alone. This is a weak justification for discarding Option 2. Third, the pension switch research report [P13][P16] shows the recommended solution was selected and ranked within the Defaqto system, but the specific ranking comparison (whether other solutions ranked higher by maturity value) is not reproduced in the suitability report, and no explanation is given if a higher-ranked solution was available.\n\nThe fact find employer pension entry [P24] records 'Does your employer currently operate a pension scheme: Yes' and the client is not a member but may become eligible. The report treats Option 4 as not applicable because the client has retired. This inconsistency is unlikely to be material — the suitability report consistently describes Anthony as recently retired, and the fact find entry almost certainly relates to Yvette's employment — but it remains unexplained. On vulnerability: no vulnerability assessment is present in the file (noted as absent in the extraction), and the report does not flag any vulnerability characteristics that would affect this analysis. No adjustment to the finding is therefore required on vulnerability grounds.\n\nOverall, the safeguarded benefits and penalty analysis is adequately addressed and evidenced. The main concern is the weak, inferred justification for discarding Option 2 without quantification, and the potential that the switch comparison projections were run against the wrong existing fund — which would undermine the evidential basis for the '£0 effect of switching' claim.","citations":[{"passageId":"P5","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"Lower Costs - Moving to our agency terms will reduce your platform charge from 0.32% to 0.21%.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"Risk Alignment - The current funds have a risk level of 6, which is too high for your agreed Cautious Balanced risk profile of 4. Switching to the recommended fund will ensure your investments match your risk tolerance.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report 16-12-2025 10-06.md","category":"G","quote":"No benefits were listed for the existing products","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Pension switch report 16-12-2025 10-06.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing pension:","£79,092.32"]},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Pension switch report 16-12-2025 10-06.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["New solution day one:","£79,092.32"]},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Pension switch report 16-12-2025 10-06.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva Insured Funds Multi-asset Plus Fund II (GBP)","Aviva Life & Pensions UK Ltd","GB00B8BV3X17","","","","100.00"]},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Personal Pension Plan review report 03-12-2025 1